      "id": "ES-C381-lavar",
      "sequence": 7970,
      "title": "lavar — a washing-place, and a downpour",
      "headword": "lavar",
      "romanization": "lavar",
      "gloss": "to wash — Latin lavare, which English kept in lavatory, launder and lavish",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b223af71950005d9",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say last, then necessary. (Último; necesario.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el agua — what you wash with."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la cara — one thing you wash."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la-VAR, two beats with the weight on the second. The v is the same sound as b, and the final r is a single tap. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "lavar — \"to wash.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin lavare meant to wash, and that is the whole of the Spanish story. Said like that it is a circle: the word for washing comes from the word for washing. So the interesting part is not where lavar came from. It is where else the same Latin verb went."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has it three times over, and never looking like itself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A lavatory is, literally, a washing-place. The room got its name from the basin, not the other way round — which is why the word sounds so formal for so ordinary a thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "To launder is the same verb, arrived by a longer road: Latin lavandaria, things to be washed, through French, losing a syllable on the way. The modern sense of laundering money is a metaphor somebody built on top of an already buried one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And lavish is the surprise. Before it meant generous, it meant a downpour — a lavasse, a sudden heavy rain. Spending lavishly is spending the way rain falls."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One caution, because it is the connection everyone reaches for first: lava and lavender are not safe to add to this list. Both have disputed etymologies, and the tidy story that lava is \"what washes down the mountain\" is one proposal among several rather than the answer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name the action: lavar. And you can hear it inside an English word you already knew."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "lavar",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *lavar*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "lavar la cara",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *lavar la cara*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el agua",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el agua*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which English room is named for its basin? (Lavatory.) And what did lavish mean before it meant generous? (A downpour.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C381-romper",
      "sequence": 7980,
      "title": "romper — a road is something broken through a forest",
      "headword": "romper",
      "romanization": "romper",
      "gloss": "to break — Latin rumpere, and the reason a road is called a route",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:f8bf3645fff77079",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say necessary. (Necesario.) And to wash? (Lavar.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la puerta — something breakable."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la ventana — another."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rom-PER, two beats with the weight on the second. The opening r is the strong trilled one at the start of a word. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "romper — \"to break.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin rumpere, to break or burst. Again the direct line is a circle, so take the branch instead — and this one is worth the walk."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The past participle of rumpere was rupta, \"broken.\" Roman Latin had the phrase rupta via: a broken way. It meant a path forced through a forest by breaking the trees down, as opposed to a road that was built."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Drop the via, keep the adjective, and rupta becomes French route and then English route. Every route you have ever taken is, etymologically, something somebody smashed a way through."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English also kept the verb straight, in rupture, erupt and abrupt — an abrupt ending is one that breaks off."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A false friend to refuse: romp has nothing to do with romper. It is a much later English word of unclear origin, and the resemblance is an accident."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name the action: romper. And you know why a route is not a built road."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "romper",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *romper*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "romper la ventana",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *romper la ventana*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "lavar la puerta",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *lavar la puerta*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What was a rupta via? (A way broken through a forest.) Is English romp related? (No — an accident.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
