        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say to jump. (Saltar.) And to break? (Romper.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la cama — where you do it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la noche — when."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "so-ÑAR, two beats with the weight on the second. The ñ is one sound, the ny of canyon, not an n followed by a y. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "soñar — \"to dream.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin somnus was sleep; somniare was to dream. Spanish took the verb."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at what happened to the m. Somniare has mn in the middle, and Spanish would not keep that cluster — the two nasals collapsed into a single new sound, and the letter invented to write it was ñ. The tilde itself began as a small n written above the line by scribes saving parchment. So the mark on top of soñar is, quite literally, the second nasal of the Latin word, moved upstairs."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English kept somnus rather than the verb. Insomnia is in- plus somnus: not-sleep. Somnolent means sleepy, and a somnambulist is a sleep-walker — somnus plus ambulare, to walk."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A false friend, and an easy one to fall into: sonar without the tilde is a different Spanish verb, meaning to sound, and English sonar is not Spanish at all — it is a modern acronym. The tilde is doing real work here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name four everyday actions: lavar, romper, saltar, soñar. Say which one you do most often."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
